Keep a folder's cached settings until its settings file has been read.

For a remote folder, FolderConfiguration starts on the copy of the folder's
settings that its last load cached, and swaps in a FileServiceBasedConfiguration
once the vscode-remote file system provider registers. The new object reads the
folder's files only when the workspace service next loads it, and until then
it holds no settings: its reparse() answers an empty model.

WorkspaceService.onDefaultConfigurationChanged reparses the folder
configuration and puts the result in place of the folder's and the workspace's
settings, without an event, since a reparse is meant to change how settings
are read rather than which ones there are. The default configuration changes
as the extensions register, since every extension that contributes settings or
configurationDefaults changes it. So whenever the read was still running when
the extensions registered, every setting of the folder's own was gone from the
configuration until the read ended, and whatever read the configuration in
between, the theme service and the extensions activating, got the user's
values instead.

FolderConfiguration now remembers which of its two configurations the
workspace service loaded last, and reparses that one: the cached copy until
the file based configuration has been loaded, which is what the workspace
service holds until then. The swap, the reads and the events are as before,
so a folder's first load, which has no cached copy, gets its settings when it
did and with the same change event.

On an Android emulator the read ends seconds after the extensions register. On
API 36 a folder whose .vscode/settings.json names Light Modern, under a user
theme of Dark Modern, showed Dark Modern for 6 to 7.5 s on every load from its
second on: from the extensions registering (code/didLoadExtensions at 12.3 s
in one load) until the read ended (Light Modern back at 20.7 s, at the moment
the explorer filled). The mirror case, a folder naming Dark Modern under Light
Modern, showed Light Modern in the same window. The page script VSCodroid adds
to the workbench page reads which theme a window shows to tell whether its
folder has a theme of its own, and a tap in that window taught it the wrong
answer, which started the next folder on the wrong theme.

Verified in headless Chromium against the web workbench of server trees built
with and without this patch, served on macOS with Dark Modern in the Machine
settings, Light Modern in the folder's, and the editor server's open of the
folder's settings.json held. Held 6 s: from the folder's second load on, the
unpatched tree showed Dark Modern from the extensions registering, at 0.4 s,
until 12.2 s, and on the third load still at 14 s; the patched tree showed
Light Modern from the extensions registering on, and the same with the two
themes swapped. A first load applied the folder's theme at 12.8 s patched and
12.7 s unpatched, and held 0.3, 0.8 and 1.5 s at 1.4, 2.3 and 3.7 s patched.

A first version of this patch swapped only once the new object had read the
files. That moved the drop rather than removing it: the settings it had read
then reached the configuration through a reparse, without an event, before the
workspace service's own load compared them, so on a folder's first load
nothing listening for configuration changes heard of them. A new folder naming
Light Modern stayed on Dark Modern on the emulator, and in Chromium with the
open held 0.3 s or 1.5 s.

One note for whoever tidies this later. The reparse is written as
`(this.loadedFolderConfiguration ?? this.folderConfiguration).reparse(` because
that text, with the spaces gone, is the token patches/fingerprints.txt matches
to prove this patch reached the packaged bundle; property names survive the
bundler and locals do not. If you hoist it into a local, move that row to
something the new shape introduces and check the replacement occurs zero times
in an unpatched bundle first.

diff --git a/src/vs/workbench/services/configuration/browser/configuration.ts b/src/vs/workbench/services/configuration/browser/configuration.ts
index a83c04f..aeb45e3 100644
--- a/src/vs/workbench/services/configuration/browser/configuration.ts
+++ b/src/vs/workbench/services/configuration/browser/configuration.ts
@@ -1018,6 +1018,7 @@ export class FolderConfiguration extends Disposable {
 	readonly onDidChange: Event<void> = this._onDidChange.event;
 
 	private folderConfiguration: CachedFolderConfiguration | FileServiceBasedConfiguration;
+	private loadedFolderConfiguration: CachedFolderConfiguration | FileServiceBasedConfiguration | undefined;
 	private readonly scopes: ConfigurationScope[];
 	private readonly configurationFolder: URI;
 	private cachedFolderConfiguration: CachedFolderConfiguration;
@@ -1053,7 +1054,11 @@ export class FolderConfiguration extends Disposable {
 	}
 
 	loadConfiguration(): Promise<ConfigurationModel> {
-		return this.folderConfiguration.loadConfiguration();
+		const folderConfiguration = this.folderConfiguration;
+		return folderConfiguration.loadConfiguration().then(configurationModel => {
+			this.loadedFolderConfiguration = folderConfiguration;
+			return configurationModel;
+		});
 	}
 
 	updateWorkspaceTrust(trusted: boolean): ConfigurationModel {
@@ -1062,7 +1067,11 @@ export class FolderConfiguration extends Disposable {
 	}
 
 	reparse(): ConfigurationModel {
-		const configurationModel = this.folderConfiguration.reparse({ scopes: this.scopes, skipRestricted: this.isUntrusted() });
+		// Reparse the configuration whose settings were last loaded, which are the ones in use.
+		// The file based configuration is swapped in before it has read the folder's files and
+		// holds no settings until then, so reparsing it, as every change to the default
+		// configuration does, would drop the folder's settings until the read ends.
+		const configurationModel = (this.loadedFolderConfiguration ?? this.folderConfiguration).reparse({ scopes: this.scopes, skipRestricted: this.isUntrusted() });
 		this.updateCache();
 		return configurationModel;
 	}
